Behaviorally, the Mangrove Cuckoo is known for its habit of mimicking the calls of other birds, particularly those of cuckoos, as a form of camouflage or to confuse potential predators. It is often found perched on branches in mangrove forests, where it blends well into the environment due to its cryptic coloration.

In summary, the Mangrove Cuckoo can be distinguished by its grayish-brown upperparts, yellowish-buff underparts, black and white barred wings, long tail, and mimicry behavior.
