Behaviorally, Swainson Warblers are known for their loud, high-pitched song, often described as "chirp-chirp-chirp" or "tseep-tseep-tseep." They are active and agile, often seen flitting through dense vegetation in search of insects. They are migratory birds, breeding in North America and wintering in South America.

Key identifying traits include its olive-green upperparts, yellowish-green underparts, and distinctive brownish cap on the head. The long, thin bill and short tail further differentiate it from other warbler species.
